diff --git a/docs/kubernetes/setup.mdx b/docs/kubernetes/setup.mdx
index a053a8f70957a5583136ce938a6ec678f92495c8..89e30e22a5d784d59c00379d2f724adfa4690908 100644
--- a/docs/kubernetes/setup.mdx
+++ b/docs/kubernetes/setup.mdx
@@ -445,6 +445,26 @@ helm upgrade --install openshell \
 
 The ServiceAccount must already have the Role and ClusterRole bindings described above.
 
+### Limit cluster discovery to a file-backed operator workspace
+
+With chart-managed cluster RBAC and a separate workspace release, set
+`rbac.clusterScoped.operatorDiscoveryNamespace` to that workspace namespace.
+Use operator mode with `operator_namespace_file` and a named
+`default_runtime_class_name`. Keep the workspace name in the file and the Helm
+value in sync.
+
+The gateway can get its release namespace, the selected workspace namespace,
+and the named RuntimeClass. It can list and watch sandboxes across the cluster
+and create TokenReviews. The existing ClusterRoleBinding selects the gateway
+ServiceAccount. Leave the value unset to use the standard role, for example
+when GPU capacity checks need to list nodes.
+
+If you set `supervisor.network.additionalCaConfigMapName`, first bind a Role in
+the selected workspace namespace to the gateway ServiceAccount. Grant `get` and
+`create` on core `configmaps` so the driver can validate or create the sandbox
+destination CA ConfigMap. The stock workspace Role omits these permissions.
+Leave the CA setting empty if you cannot grant these permissions.
+
 ### Installing without cluster-admin
 
 By default the release creates the `ClusterRole` and `ClusterRoleBinding`, so
